 "label": "PROVISIONAL_ASR_NOT_LISTENING",
 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 2.74,
   "text": "Space in Earth orbit seems cold because it's so empty.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 3.24,
   "end": 8.08,
   "text": "Without a warm environment around you, you lose heat by radiation much faster than normal.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 8.42,
   "end": 13.74,
   "text": "But space in Earth orbit is actually warm. The pedantic reason for this is that temperature is",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 13.74,
   "end": 17.64,
   "text": "a measure of the average kinetic energy of a collection of particles. And in space near the",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 17.64,
   "end": 22.3,
   "text": "Earth, molecules can have average kinetic energies in the thousands of degrees. This doesn't make",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 22.3,
   "end": 26.72,
   "text": "space feel warm, though. When I was a kid, I remember watching my dad use a metal grinder.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.106,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.0
  },
  {
   "start": 26.72,
   "end": 30.7,
   "text": "Whenever metal touched the grinding wheel, sparks flew everywhere, sometimes falling in a shower",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 30.7,
   "end": 34.74,
   "text": "on his hands and clothes. I couldn't understand why they didn't hurt him. After all, the glowing",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 34.74,
   "end": 38.66,
   "text": "sparks were several thousand degrees. I later learned that the reason the sparks didn't hurt",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 38.66,
   "end": 41.98,
   "text": "him was that they were tiny. The heat they carried could be absorbed into the body without",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 41.98,
   "end": 46.36,
   "text": "warming anything more than a tiny patch of skin. The hot molecules in space are like the sparks",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 46.36,
   "end": 50.68,
   "text": "in my dad's machine shop. They might be hot or cold, but they're so small and there are so few",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 50.68,
   "end": 54.24,
   "text": "of them they don't change your temperature very much. Instead, your heating and cooling is",
   "avg_logprob": -0.064,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.025
  },
  {
   "start": 54.28,
   "end": 57.66,
   "text": "dominated by how much heat you produce and how quickly it pours out of you into the void.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.068,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.047
  },
  {
   "start": 58.18,
   "end": 62.02,
   "text": "And this is the practical reason that space is warm. Without air or water around you to",
   "avg_logprob": -0.068,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.047
  },
  {
   "start": 62.02,
   "end": 65.22,
   "text": "carry heat away from your surface, you don't lose heat by conduction or convection,",
   "avg_logprob": -0.068,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.047
  },
  {
   "start": 65.46,
   "end": 68.74,
   "text": "just radiation, which often isn't a very effective way to cool down.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.068,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.047
  },
  {
   "start": 69.2,
   "end": 73.16,
   "text": "For most human-carrying spacecraft, the big problem isn't staying warm, it's keeping cool.",
   "avg_logprob": -0.068,
   "no_speech_prob": 0.047
  }
 ],
